The bug is a classic off-by-one error in a Go loop: using `i <= len(s)` instead of `i < len(s)`. For a slice of length `N`, valid indices run `0..N-1`. When the loop condition is `i <= len(s)`, `i` reaches `len(s)` and accessing `s[i]` causes an **index-out-of-range panic**.

**Buggy code (`main.go`):**

```go
// BUG: i <= len(s) causes panic when i == len(s)
for i := 0; i <= len(s); i++ {
    result[i] = s[i]  // panic: runtime error: index out of range [5] with length 4
}
```

**Fixed code:**

```go
// FIX: i < len(s) stops at the last valid index len(s)-1
for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
    result[i] = s[i]  // safe: i ∈ [0, len(s)-1]
}
```

