The package `/home/kara/off-by-one/` demonstrates three classic off-by-one errors in Go, each caught by a targeted self-test:

### Bug 1: `Chunk` — Loop condition `i <= len(s)`

```go
// BEFORE (broken)
for i := 0; i <= len(s); i += size {
    ...
    chunks = append(chunks, s[i:end])
}

// AFTER (fixed)
for i := 0; i < len(s); i += size {
    ...
}
```

When `len(s)` is evenly divisible by `size`, the loop runs one extra iteration at `i = len(s)`, appending an empty chunk `[]`. The test `TestChunk_ExactFit` caught this: expected `[[1 2 3] [4 5 6]]`, got `[[1 2 3] [4 5 6] []]`.

### Bug 2: `Paginate` — Missing bounds clamp on `end`

```go
// BEFORE (broken)
return items[start:end], nil  // panics when end > len(items)

// AFTER (fixed)
if end > len(items) {
    end = len(items)
}
return items[start:end], nil
```

When requesting a partial last page (e.g., page 2 of 3 items from 5 elements), `end = 6` exceeds `len(items) = 5`, causing a runtime panic. The test `TestPaginate_LastPagePartial` caught this with a slice bounds out-of-range panic.

### Bug 3: `InclusiveRange` — Loop condition `i < end`

```go
