**Off-by-one principle**: A programming error where a loop iterates one time too many or one time too few, or where an index is off by ±1 from the correct value. It most commonly arises from using `<=` instead of `<` in loop conditions, using `len` instead of `len-1` for the last index, or forgetting the `+1` when computing inclusive ranges.

### Real example: Summing array elements

```go
// BUG: i <= n reads one element past the end
func SumFirstN_buggy(nums []int, n int) int {
    sum := 0
    for i := 0; i <= n; i++ {  // should be i < n
        sum += nums[i]         // panics when n == len(nums)
    }
    return sum
}

// FIX: i < n stops before the out-of-bounds access
func SumFirstN_fixed(nums []int, n int) int {
    sum := 0
    for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
        sum += nums[i]
    }
    return sum
}
```

### Other common forms of off-by-one:

| Pattern | Buggy | Fixed | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| Loop over slice | `for i := 0; i <= len(s); i++` | `for i := 0; i < len(s); i++` | Index `len(s)` is out of bounds |
| Slice first n chars | `s[:n-1]` | `s[:n]` | At n=0, `s[:-1]` wraps around or panics |
| Inclusive range count | `b - a` | `b - a + 1` | Days 1–3 inclusive = 3 days, not 2 |
| Pagination | `total / perPage` | `(total + perPage - 1) / perPage` | 25 items @ 10/page = 3 pages, not 2 |
| Last index | `s[len(s)]` | `s[len(s)-1]` | Indices are 0-based, last is len-1 |

