The classic off-by-one error occurs when a loop condition uses `<=` instead of `<` (or vice versa), causing an index to exceed the slice bounds. In Go, this produces an `index out of range [n] with length n` panic at runtime.

**Buggy code (`offbyone.go`):** The condition `i <= n` iterates one time too many:

```go
func SumFirstN(s []int, n int) int {
	sum := 0
	for i := 0; i <= n; i++ { // BUG: should be i < n
		sum += s[i]
	}
	return sum
}
```

When `s = []int{10, 20, 30}` and `n = 3`, the loop accesses `s[3]` which is out of bounds (valid indices are 0, 1, 2).

**Fixed code:** Change `<=` to `<`:

```go
func SumFirstNFixed(s []int, n int) int {
	sum := 0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ { // FIXED
		sum += s[i]
	}
	return sum
}
```

**Robust code (`robust.go`):** Also clamp `n` to `len(s)` to handle `n > len(s)`:

```go
func SumFirstNClamped(s []int, n int) int {
	if n > len(s) {
		n = len(s)
	}
	sum := 0
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		sum += s[i]
	}
	return sum
}
```

