The classic off-by-one error occurs when a loop or index uses `<=` instead of `<`, causing access one index past the valid range. In Go, this produces a runtime panic: `index out of range`.

**Bug:**
```go
// Off-by-one: iterates i = 0..len(nums) inclusive — goes one too far
func sumSliceBroken(nums []int) int {
    sum := 0
    for i := 0; i <= len(nums); i++ {
        sum += nums[i]  // panics when i == len(nums)
    }
    return sum
}
```

**Fix:**
```go
// Correct: iterates i = 0..len(nums)-1 — stays within bounds
func sumSliceFixed(nums []int) int {
    sum := 0
    for i := 0; i < len(nums); i++ {
        sum += nums[i]
    }
    return sum
}
```

This single-character change (`<=` → `<`) eliminates the panic and correctly sums all elements.

**Other common off-by-one patterns (and their fixes):**

| Pattern | Bug | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Loop bound | `for i := 0; i <= n; i++` | `for i := 0; i < n; i++` |
| Slice slicing | `s[:len(s)]` (fine) vs `s[:len(s)+1]` | `s[:len(s)]` |
| Descending loop | `for i := n; i >= 0; i--` accessing `arr[i]` at `i` | Start at `n-1`: `for i := n-1; i >= 0; i--` |
| Inclusive upper bound check | `if index > len(arr)` | `if index >= len(arr)` |

